      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the effect of a 4-month high-intensity interval training programme on cognitive functioning, cerebral oxygenation, central haemodynamic and cardiometabolic parameters and aerobic capacity in obese patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Cognitive functioning, cerebral oxygenation, central haemodynamic, cardiometabolic and exercise para-meters were measured before and after a 4-month high-intensity interval training programme in 6 obese patients (mean age 49 years (standard deviation 8), fat mass percentage 31\u2009±\u20097%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Body composition (body mass, total and trunk fat mass, waist circumference) and fasting insulin were improved after the programme (p\u2009<\u20090.05). V.\u2009O2 and power output at ventilatory threshold and peak power output were improved after the programme (p\u2009<\u20090.05). Cognitive functioning, including short-term and verbal memory, attention and processing speed, was significantly improved after training (p\u2009<\u20090.05). Cerebral oxygen extraction was also improved after training (p\u2009<\u20090.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These preliminary results indicate that a 4-month high-intensity interval training programme in obese patients improved both cognitive functioning and cere-bral oxygen extraction, in association with improved exercise capacity and body composition."
        }
      ]},
